1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,544
Problem nine is a quotient.

2
00:00:01,814 --> 00:00:04,786
The denominator changes, so we need the quotient rule.

3
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:09,515
The original function is defined only where one plus cosine x is not zero.

4
00:00:10,635 --> 00:00:16,904
Use bottom times derivative of top, minus top times derivative of bottom, over bottom squared.

5
00:00:17,174 --> 00:00:19,937
The derivative of cosine is negative sine.

6
00:00:20,207 --> 00:00:23,040
Notice the two minus signs in the second product.

7
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:26,296
The double negative becomes a plus.

8
00:00:26,566 --> 00:00:30,247
Now use sine squared plus cosine squared equals one.

9
00:00:30,517 --> 00:00:33,315
This identity simplifies the numerator.

10
00:00:34,435 --> 00:00:37,291
Cancel one factor of one plus cosine x.

11
00:00:37,561 --> 00:00:40,045
Keep the restriction from the original function.

12
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:44,193
The most common error here is losing the minus sign in the quotient rule.

13
00:00:45,313 --> 00:00:48,355
Problem ten is a product of two changing factors.

14
00:00:48,625 --> 00:00:51,202
We cannot simply multiply their derivatives.

15
00:00:51,472 --> 00:00:54,781
Name the first factor u and the second factor v.

16
00:00:55,901 --> 00:01:00,359
The product rule is u prime times v, plus u times v prime.

17
00:01:00,629 --> 00:01:04,936
Think: change one factor at a time, while keeping the other factor.

18
00:01:06,056 --> 00:01:08,506
The derivative of the first factor is two.

19
00:01:08,776 --> 00:01:16,183
For the logarithm, use the chain rule: the derivative of the inside, six x, divided by the original inside.

20
00:01:17,303 --> 00:01:19,416
Substitute into the product rule.

21
00:01:19,686 --> 00:01:21,590
Both terms are necessary.

22
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:27,073
You may leave the derivative in this factored form; expanding it does not make it more correct.

23
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:31,908
Problem eleven combines the product rule with two chain rules.

24
00:01:32,178 --> 00:01:37,159
The superscript negative one means inverse tangent, not reciprocal tangent.

25
00:01:38,279 --> 00:01:41,251
The exponential needs the inside derivative two.

26
00:01:41,521 --> 00:01:48,243
Inverse tangent gives one over one plus its inside squared, multiplied by the inside derivative one half.

27
00:01:48,513 --> 00:01:51,520
Simplifying gives two over x squared plus four.

28
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:55,845
Use the product rule and factor out e to the two x.

29
00:01:56,115 --> 00:02:00,097
There are two terms inside the brackets because either factor can change.

30
00:02:01,217 --> 00:02:05,455
Use the inverse sine derivative, not the ordinary sine derivative.

31
00:02:05,725 --> 00:02:08,267
Name the inside x squared minus one.

32
00:02:09,387 --> 00:02:12,429
Put the inside derivative two x in the numerator.

33
00:02:12,699 --> 00:02:16,844
The denominator is square root of one minus the entire inside squared.

34
00:02:17,114 --> 00:02:20,144
The formula applies where the denominator is positive.

35
00:02:21,264 --> 00:02:26,326
The original inverse sine is real when absolute x is at most square root two.

36
00:02:26,596 --> 00:02:31,507
The derivative formula requires the inside strictly between negative one and one.

37
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:36,235
Zero is excluded: the function has different one-sided slopes there.

38
00:02:37,355 --> 00:02:41,175
Problem thirteen uses theta as the independent variable.

39
00:02:41,445 --> 00:02:43,999
The rules are identical to the rules for x.

40
00:02:44,269 --> 00:02:46,881
Treat the sum in parentheses as one factor.

41
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:52,367
Differentiate tangent for the first term and the parenthesized sum for the second.

42
00:02:52,637 --> 00:02:56,363
The derivative of the sum is cosine theta minus sine theta.

43
00:02:57,483 --> 00:03:02,000
For problem fourteen, divide the numerator into x minus one plus three.

44
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:06,589
This rewriting makes both derivatives easier and keeps x not equal to one.

45
00:03:07,709 --> 00:03:09,694
One differentiates to zero.

46
00:03:09,964 --> 00:03:16,013
Three times x minus one to the negative one becomes negative three times x minus one to the negative two.

47
00:03:17,133 --> 00:03:18,549
Differentiate again.

48
00:03:18,819 --> 00:03:21,675
Negative three times negative two gives six.

49
00:03:21,945 --> 00:03:25,893
The inside derivative is one, so it adds no extra factor.

50
00:03:27,013 --> 00:03:28,522
Use the product rule.

51
00:03:28,792 --> 00:03:32,449
Both cosecant and cotangent have negative derivatives.

52
00:03:32,719 --> 00:03:35,447
This problem is a useful test of sign tracking.

53
00:03:36,567 --> 00:03:40,991
The first product contributes negative cosecant times cotangent squared.

54
00:03:41,261 --> 00:03:44,094
The second contributes negative cosecant cubed.

55
00:03:45,214 --> 00:03:49,266
For the second derivative, apply the product rule again to the first term.

56
00:03:49,536 --> 00:03:55,596
Differentiating cotangent squared uses the chain rule and contributes a second positive term.

57
00:03:56,716 --> 00:04:02,347
The derivative of negative cosecant cubed contributes another three cosecant cubed cotangent.

58
00:04:02,617 --> 00:04:05,531
Combine that with the two already present to get five.

59
00:04:06,651 --> 00:04:08,845
Differentiate with respect to u.

60
00:04:09,115 --> 00:04:14,096
The top derivative is negative two u, and the bottom derivative is positive two u.

61
00:04:15,216 --> 00:04:18,176
The quotient rule subtracts the second product.

62
00:04:18,446 --> 00:04:22,150
Keep the parentheses so the minus sign applies to the entire product.

63
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:26,405
Expanding the numerator cancels the cubic terms.

64
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:29,182
The two linear terms add to negative four u.

65
00:04:29,452 --> 00:04:32,123
The denominator never vanishes for real u.

66
00:04:33,243 --> 00:04:35,704
Rewrite the square root as a one-half power.

67
00:04:35,974 --> 00:04:40,920
The outer derivative must be multiplied by the derivative of the polynomial inside.

68
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:44,826
The inside derivative is two x minus seven.

69
00:04:45,096 --> 00:04:52,353
The formula applies when x squared minus seven x is positive, meaning x less than zero or x greater than seven.

70
00:04:52,623 --> 00:04:55,920
The endpoints do not have a finite two-sided derivative.

71
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,919
Multiply powers of x by adding exponents.

72
00:05:00,189 --> 00:05:06,563
The first term has exponent one plus one half, and the reciprocal term has exponent negative two minus one half.

73
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:08,865
The real domain is x positive.

74
00:05:09,985 --> 00:05:12,144
Use the power rule on each term.

75
00:05:12,414 --> 00:05:17,453
This is simpler than using both a product rule and a quotient rule on the original expression.

76
00:05:18,573 --> 00:05:23,786
Identify the outside power, three halves, and the inside t minus one over t.

77
00:05:24,056 --> 00:05:26,680
The chain rule multiplies their derivatives.

78
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:32,269
The derivative of negative one over t is positive one over t squared.

79
00:05:32,539 --> 00:05:33,956
Keep that plus sign.

80
00:05:34,226 --> 00:05:39,172
This formula is used on the interior of the real domain where the inside is positive.

81
00:05:40,292 --> 00:05:42,904
The whole polynomial is raised to the fourth power.

82
00:05:43,174 --> 00:05:45,821
Keep it together while taking the outer derivative.

83
00:05:46,941 --> 00:05:50,087
Bring down four and lower the outer exponent to three.

84
00:05:50,357 --> 00:05:55,152
Then multiply by three x squared minus five, the derivative of the inside.

85
00:05:56,272 --> 00:05:59,883
This is a product, and each factor also needs the chain rule.

86
00:06:00,153 --> 00:06:03,172
Write two product-rule terms before simplifying.

87
00:06:04,292 --> 00:06:08,494
The first factor differentiates to three times its square times twelve t.

88
00:06:08,764 --> 00:06:12,839
The second differentiates to four times its cube times three t squared.

89
00:06:13,109 --> 00:06:15,373
Keep the untouched factor in each term.

90
00:06:16,493 --> 00:06:18,374
This factored form is optional.

91
00:06:18,644 --> 00:06:21,268
Pull out the factors shared by both terms.

92
00:06:21,538 --> 00:06:25,776
The unsimplified two-term version is also a complete correct derivative.

93
00:06:26,896 --> 00:06:29,287
The independent variable is y here.

94
00:06:29,557 --> 00:06:34,016
We need the chain rule for the outer cube and the quotient rule for the inside fraction.

95
00:06:35,136 --> 00:06:41,359
The numerator simplifies to thirteen because y cancels and subtracting negative six adds six.

96
00:06:42,479 --> 00:06:46,612
Multiply the derivative of the outer cube by the derivative of the inside.

97
00:06:46,882 --> 00:06:49,819
Combining the denominator powers gives a fourth power.

98
00:06:50,089 --> 00:06:51,993
Y cannot be negative seven.

99
00:06:53,113 --> 00:06:58,709
There are multiple layers here: a square root outside, and two exponentials inside.

100
00:06:58,979 --> 00:07:01,928
Differentiate the entire expression under the radical.

101
00:07:03,048 --> 00:07:05,242
The first exponent contributes four.

102
00:07:05,512 --> 00:07:08,090
The second exponent contributes two x.

103
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:10,600
Keep the subtraction between the terms.

104
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:15,633
Divide the inside derivative by twice the original square root.

105
00:07:15,903 --> 00:07:20,675
For a finite derivative this requires the expression under the radical to be positive.

106
00:07:20,945 --> 00:07:23,928
Here that means zero less than x less than four.

107
00:07:25,048 --> 00:07:27,835
The powers apply to the trigonometric values.

108
00:07:28,105 --> 00:07:31,390
Sine cubed x means sine x, all cubed.

109
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:34,041
Differentiate each cube with the chain rule.

110
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:38,736
The sine term contributes cosine as its inside derivative.

111
00:07:39,006 --> 00:07:43,372
The cosine term contributes negative sine, which creates the minus sign.

112
00:07:44,492 --> 00:07:48,369
Factor out three sine x cosine x if you want a compact answer.

113
00:07:48,639 --> 00:07:51,240
Both forms describe the same derivative.

114
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:56,644
Problem twenty five means the square of sine of cosine of k x.

115
00:07:56,914 --> 00:07:58,273
Treat k as a constant.

116
00:07:58,543 --> 00:08:00,899
Work from the outside toward the inside.

117
00:08:02,019 --> 00:08:04,109
The outermost operation is squaring.

118
00:08:04,379 --> 00:08:10,509
Its derivative is two times the unchanged sine, multiplied by the derivative of that sine.

119
00:08:11,629 --> 00:08:15,310
The derivative of sine u is cosine u times u prime.

120
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:19,097
We still owe a derivative for the cosine layer and for k x.

121
00:08:20,217 --> 00:08:22,795
Cosine differentiates to negative sine.

122
00:08:23,065 --> 00:08:25,921
Then the inside k x differentiates to k.

123
00:08:26,191 --> 00:08:28,954
Every changing layer contributes one factor.

124
00:08:30,074 --> 00:08:31,804
Put the layers back together.

125
00:08:32,074 --> 00:08:35,754
Read the factors from outside to inside to check that none is missing.

126
00:08:36,024 --> 00:08:39,856
In particular, remember both the negative sign and the factor k.

127
00:08:40,976 --> 00:08:45,109
Use the product rule for x times hyperbolic sine of x squared.

128
00:08:45,379 --> 00:08:50,766
Hyperbolic sine differentiates to hyperbolic cosine, with an additional inside derivative.

129
00:08:51,886 --> 00:08:53,767
The derivative of x is one.

130
00:08:54,037 --> 00:08:59,621
In the second term, keep x and multiply hyperbolic cosine of x squared by two x.

131
00:09:00,741 --> 00:09:02,343
Combine the x factors.

132
00:09:02,613 --> 00:09:06,572
There is no negative sign when differentiating hyperbolic sine.

133
00:09:07,692 --> 00:09:13,068
For a logarithm of a function, differentiate the inside and divide by the original inside.

134
00:09:13,338 --> 00:09:17,192
Then use the chain rule again for hyperbolic cosine of three x.

135
00:09:18,312 --> 00:09:23,688
Hyperbolic cosine differentiates to hyperbolic sine, and three x contributes three.

136
00:09:23,958 --> 00:09:28,114
Hyperbolic sine divided by hyperbolic cosine is hyperbolic tangent.
